    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Evaluate $\\sqrt{25}\\times\\sqrt{81}$ and $\\sqrt{25\\times81}$. Are they equal?",
        "solution_text": "$\\sqrt{25}\\times\\sqrt{81}=5\\times9=45$.\n$\\sqrt{25\\times81}=\\sqrt{2025}=45$, since $45^2=2025$.\nThe values are equal. This numerical check agrees with $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{b}=\\sqrt{ab}$ for nonnegative $a$ and $b$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Use the product rule to evaluate.\n(a) $\\sqrt{16\\times121}$\n(b) $\\sqrt{7}\\times\\sqrt{28}$",
        "solution_text": "(a) Split the product under the root.\n$\\sqrt{16\\times121}=\\sqrt{16}\\times\\sqrt{121}=4\\times11=44$.\n(b) Join the two roots and multiply the numbers under them.\n$\\sqrt{7}\\times\\sqrt{28}=\\sqrt{7\\times28}=\\sqrt{196}=14$.\nNeither factor in (b) is a whole number, but their product is."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Write each expression as a single square root.\n(a) $\\sqrt{11}\\times\\sqrt{2}$\n(b) $\\sqrt{3}\\times\\sqrt{5}\\times\\sqrt{2}$",
        "solution_text": "(a) $\\sqrt{11}\\times\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{11\\times2}=\\sqrt{22}$.\n(b) Apply the product rule twice.\n$\\sqrt{3}\\times\\sqrt{5}\\times\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{15}\\times\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{30}$.\nKeep these exact answers as single roots."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Use the product rule to evaluate $\\sqrt{4\\times9\\times49}$.",
        "solution_text": "Split into three known square roots.\n$\\sqrt{4\\times9\\times49}=\\sqrt{4}\\times\\sqrt{9}\\times\\sqrt{49}=2\\times3\\times7=42$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Evaluate each pair. Do the results support a similar rule for addition or subtraction?\n(a) $\\sqrt{16}+\\sqrt{9}$ and $\\sqrt{16+9}$\n(b) $\\sqrt{25}-\\sqrt{9}$ and $\\sqrt{25-9}$",
        "solution_text": "(a) $\\sqrt{16}+\\sqrt{9}=4+3=7$, but $\\sqrt{16+9}=\\sqrt{25}=5$.\n(b) $\\sqrt{25}-\\sqrt{9}=5-3=2$, but $\\sqrt{25-9}=\\sqrt{16}=4$.\nThe answers differ in each pair. The product rule does not give a general rule for joining roots across addition or subtraction."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Find the nonnegative number in each box.\n(a) $\\sqrt{8}\\times\\sqrt{\\square}=\\sqrt{200}$\n(b) $\\sqrt{10}\\times\\sqrt{\\square}=20$",
        "solution_text": "(a) Join the roots: $\\sqrt{8\\times\\square}=\\sqrt{200}$.\nThe number under the root must be $200$, so $8\\times\\square=200$.\n$\\square=200\\div8=25$.\n(b) Join the roots: $\\sqrt{10\\times\\square}=20$.\nSince $20^2=400$, $10\\times\\square=400$.\n$\\square=400\\div10=40$.\nCheck: $\\sqrt{10}\\times\\sqrt{40}=\\sqrt{400}=20$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A rectangle has width $\\sqrt{5}$ cm and length $\\sqrt{20}$ cm. Find its area as a basic numeral.",
        "solution_text": "Area is width multiplied by length.\n$\\sqrt{5}\\times\\sqrt{20}=\\sqrt{5\\times20}=\\sqrt{100}=10$.\nThe area is $10$ cm$^2$."
      }
    ],
